No Sorting Needed! From O(n log n) to O(n) | Single Traversal + Euclid's Algorithm

Solution 
https://leetcode.com/problems/find-greatest-common-divisor-of-array/solutions/8404763/no-sorting-needed-from-on-log-n-to-on-si-6zyu

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

INTUITION

A straightforward approach is to sort the array so the smallest and largest
elements appear at the beginning and end. While this works, sorting takes
O(n log n) time, which is unnecessary because we only need the minimum and
maximum values.

Instead, we find both values in a single traversal and compute their GCD
using Euclid's Algorithm, reducing the overall complexity to O(n).

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

APPROACH

1. Initialize `small` and `big` with the first element.
2. Traverse the array once:
   • Update `small` if a smaller element is found.
   • Update `big` if a larger element is found.
3. Compute the GCD using Euclid's Algorithm.
4. Return the result.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

COMPLEXITY

Time  : O(n + log(min(small, big))) ≈ O(n)
Space : O(1)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

CODE

```java
class Solution {
    public int findGCD(int[] nums) {
        int small = nums[0];
        int big = nums[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] < small) small = nums[i];
            if (nums[i] > big) big = nums[i];
        }

        return gcd(small, big);
    }

    private int gcd(int a, int b) {
        while (b != 0) {
            int temp = b;
            b = a % b;
            a = temp;
        }
        return a;
    }
}
